Kernel 4 — Reveal, Role-Filtered Cave UI, and First Illusion Layer
Purpose

Complete the first true theater trick in browser UI:

same cave session
different users see different things
controls differ by role
reveal changes audience perception in real time

This kernel is about illusion, not infrastructure.

Scope

Build only what is required to prove:

fire is visible to producer/director/actor by default
fire is hidden from audience by default
reveal/hide changes audience view live
cave UI differs by role
actor speech and audience reaction continue working
Assumptions

Already exists:

browser map
venue icons on map
venue routing
cave page
audition hall page
signup/login/session auth
role and venue access floor
workshop asset upload floor
shared cave chat/session proof

This kernel should not rebuild those.

Required behavior
Default visibility

For first-fire in the-cave:

Producer: visible
Director: visible
Actor/Cast: visible
Audience: hidden
Reveal behavior

A reveal action changes audience visibility forward in time.

Hide behavior

A hide action can conceal it again during the same session.

Policy toggle

Production-level boolean:

actors_can_reveal

Rules:

false → only director (and producer) can reveal/hide
true → actor may reveal/hide too

First implementation may be a simple backend/config value. No policy editor required.

Cave UI by role
Audience UI

Show:

stage viewport
speech display
reaction controls

Do not show:

reveal/hide controls
backstage state
hidden element indicators
Actor UI

Show:

stage viewport
speak controls
reveal/hide controls only if policy allows
minimal awareness of hidden/visible fire state
Director UI

Show:

stage viewport
speak controls if permitted
reveal/hide controls
current fire visibility state
current actors_can_reveal state (read-only is acceptable)
Producer UI

For now:

may inherit director UI
Rendering requirements

Use the existing frontend shell and cave page.

Inside cave:

render actual fire asset
render speech overlay/text
render reactions
do not render fire for audience until revealed

No drag/drop.
No layout editor.
No animation system required beyond simple show/hide.

Actions required

At minimum:

perform/speak
react/emote
direct/reveal_element
direct/hide_element

If you prefer narrower naming for this kernel, that is acceptable, but the behavior must be equivalent.

Persistence expectations

Reveal/hide state must survive:

browser refresh
rejoin to current session

This can be implemented through:

persisted action replay
persisted derived state
or equivalent server-truth mechanism

But it must not be client-local illusion only.

Non-goals

Do not build in Kernel 4:

note cards
audition approval loop
producer control panels
workshop UI improvements
drag/drop positioning
black box venue logic
dynamic audience segments
comments
ticketing / will-call
map redesign
3D surfaces
Success criteria

Kernel 4 passes when:

actor and audience both enter the-cave
actor sees fire immediately
audience does not
actor or director reveals fire
audience sees fire in real time
actor or director hides fire
audience loses fire again in real time
actor speech still works
audience reaction still works
cave controls visibly differ by role
Recommended implementation stance

Keep it narrow.

Do not generalize the whole view engine yet.

A valid first implementation is:

one element: first-fire
one boolean visibility state for audience
role-based conditional rendering in cave UI
one production policy boolean: actors_can_reveal

That is enough to prove the illusion layer without overbuilding.

Assumed current frontend baseline
map GUI already exists
venue pages are reachable from the map
session-aware user greeting already exists
cave session already supports shared real-time speech

This prevents the next builder from wasting time rebuilding shell work.